WEBVTT
TITLE C Fundamentals: Types, Operators and Control Flow — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
A type is not a size. It is a promise about what the compiler will let you store.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.000
The standard guarantees char is one byte, and that the rank order char < short < int < long is respected.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000
The range follows from the width: an n-bit unsigned type runs 0 to 2^n - 1, and signed runs -2^(n-1) to 2^(n-1) - 1.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.000
char is the awkward one: it is a distinct type, and whether it is signed is the implementation's choice.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.000
A literal is not untyped. The way you write it decides the type before any operator sees it.

6
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:42.000
42 is int. Adding u makes it unsigned, adding L makes it long, and 42LL is long long.

7
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.000
3.0 is a double even though it looks like an int, and 3.0f is the float. The default is double.

8
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.000
In C a character constant has type int, so sizeof('a') is 4, not 1. The string "a" is a char array of length 2.

9
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.500
char and short are narrower than int, so in arithmetic they are first converted to int.

10
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:11.000
Lifting a signed char copies the sign bit upwards. Lifting an unsigned char pads with zeros.

11
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:18.500
So char c = 200 stores -56 on a signed-char machine, and c + 1 is the int -55.

12
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:26.000
unsigned char u = 200 is exactly 200, and u + 1 is 201. Same pattern, different declared type.

13
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Two operands of different types are converted to one common type before the operator runs.

14
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:42.000
For two integers the rule is short: promote both, then the wider type wins, and unsigned beats signed of equal rank.

15
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:50.000
So in -1 < 1u the -1 becomes 4294967295, and the comparison is false.

16
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.000
The same conversion is behind the warning on -1 < sizeof(int): sizeof has type size_t, which is unsigned.

17
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Unsigned arithmetic is defined to be modulo 2^n, where n is the number of value bits.

18
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:12.000
So an unsigned char holding 250, plus 10, is 4. The odometer rolls past 255 and continues at 0.

19
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Signed overflow is different: the standard says the behaviour is undefined, so there is no answer to give.

20
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Keep the two apart: unsigned wrap is a rule you can compute with, signed overflow is a rule that does not exist.

21
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Integer division throws the fraction away. Since C99 the direction is fixed: truncate towards zero.

22
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:40.000
So -7/2 is -3, not -4. Minus 3.5 moves towards zero, and floor would have moved away from it.

23
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:47.000
The remainder is fixed by one identity: (a/b)*b + a%b equals a. That forces -7%2 to be -1.

24
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Two more facts: division by zero is undefined, and INT_MIN / -1 overflows, so it is undefined too.

25
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Precedence decides which operator binds first. Associativity decides the order between equals.

26
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Unary, then multiplicative, then additive, then shift, then relational, then equality, then the bitwise three, then logical.

27
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:15.000
In 2 + 3 * 4 % 5 - 1 the multiplicative pair binds first, left to right: 3*4 is 12, then 12%5 is 2.

28
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.000
So the expression is 2 + 2 - 1, which is 3. Assignment is right associative, so a = b = 0 stores 0 in b first.

29
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:29.500
&& evaluates its left operand, and stops if that operand is false. The right side is never touched.

30
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:37.000
|| is the mirror image: it stops when the left operand is true.

31
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:44.500
So if (i < n && a[i] == key) is safe: when i is out of range the array is never indexed.

32
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:52.000
Count the side effects, not the operands: in 0 && f() the call happens zero times.

33
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:00.000
while tests first, for is a while with the bookkeeping moved into the header, and do-while tests last.

34
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
for (i = 0; i < n; i++) runs exactly n times when n is positive, and zero times when n is zero.

35
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Now the trap: for (i = n; i >= 0; i--) with i declared unsigned never terminates.

36
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:24.000
break leaves the loop at once; continue jumps to the next test, skipping the rest of the body.

37
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:33.000
switch evaluates its expression once, then jumps to the matching case label.

38
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:42.000
From that label execution runs straight on into the following cases. That is fall-through, and it is the default.

39
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:51.000
With x = 1, case 1 increments a, falls into case 2 and increments b, then break stops it. c is untouched.

40
00:04:51.000 --> 00:05:00.000
A default label is optional, and it is only used when no case matches. A missing match with no default does nothing.

